{"senses": [{"examples": [{"text": "I will only lend you my car if you fill up the tank.", "bold_text_offsets": [[12, 16]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "Where is that hundred euros I lent you months ago?", "bold_text_offsets": [[30, 34]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of peer-to-peer lending comes close. This is an industry that brings together individual savers and lenders on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to lend.", "ref": "2013 June 1, “End of the peer show”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8838, London: The Economist Group, →ISSN, →OCLC, page 71:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[57, 61], [141, 145], [232, 236]]}], "categories": ["English terms with quotations", "English terms with usage examples", "English transitive verbs"], "links": [["allow", "allow"], ["temporarily", "temporarily"], ["condition", "condition"], ["equivalent", "equivalent"], ["return", "#Verb"]], "raw_glosses": ["(transitive) To allow to be used by someone temporarily, on condition that it or its equivalent will be returned."], "glosses": ["To allow to be used by someone temporarily, on condition that it or its equivalent will be returned."], "tags": ["transitive"]}, {"categories": ["English intransitive verbs"], "links": [["loan", "loan"]], "raw_glosses": ["(intransitive) To make a loan."], "glosses": ["To make a loan."], "tags": ["intransitive"]}, {"examples": [{"text": "Poems do not lend themselves to translation easily.", "bold_text_offsets": [[13, 17]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "The long history of the past does not lend itself to a simple black and white interpretation.", "bold_text_offsets": [[38, 42]], "type": "example"}], "categories": ["English reflexive verbs", "English terms with usage examples"], "links": [["suitable", "suitable"], ["applicable", "applicable"], ["fit", "fit"]], "raw_glosses": ["(reflexive) To be suitable or applicable, to fit."], "glosses": ["To be suitable or applicable, to fit."], "tags": ["reflexive"]}, {"examples": [{"text": "Can you lend me some assistance?", "bold_text_offsets": [[8, 12]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "The famous director lent his name to the new film.", "bold_text_offsets": [[20, 24]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "[…] Cato, lend me for a vvhile thy Patience, / And condeſcend to hear a young Man ſpeak.", "ref": "1712 (date written), [Joseph] Addison, Cato, a Tragedy. […], London: […] J[acob] Tonson, […], published 1713, →OCLC, Act II, scene i, page 23:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[10, 14]]}, {"text": "Mountain lines and distant horizons lend space and largeness to his compositions.", "ref": "1886, John Addington Symonds, Sir Philip Sidney:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[36, 40]]}, {"text": "FORSEA absolutely condemns the political manipulation on the part of the junta, General Prayuth, the Thai Senate and the parties that lent their support for General Prayuth that led him to parliamentary victory.", "ref": "2019 June 8, “Condemning the return to premiership of General Prayuth Chan-ocha through a manipulative method”, in forsea.co, FORSEA, retrieved 09 Jun 2019:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[134, 138]]}], "categories": ["English terms with quotations", "English terms with usage examples", "Quotation templates to be cleaned"], "glosses": ["To afford; to grant or furnish in general."]}, {"categories": ["English proscribed terms"], "links": [["borrow", "borrow"]], "raw_glosses": ["(proscribed) To borrow."], "glosses": ["To borrow."], "tags": ["proscribed"]}], "pos": "verb", "head_templates": [{"name": "en-verb", "args": {"1": "~s", "2": "~ing", "3": "lent"}, "expansion": "lend (third-person singular simple present lends, present participle lending, simple past and past participle lent)"}], "categories": ["English countable nouns", "English entries with incorrect language header", "English irregular verbs", "English lemmas", "English links with redun